Every Tuesday evening I take the bus into Birmingham and then take a 10 minute walk from the bus stop to the Birmingham Hippodrome where I attend a dance class. I do this as an opportunity to grow in my skill as dancer and to get to know some of the other dancers in the area. My hope is that I will have the opportunity to build friendships and share the gospel with them. But right now I want to share with you an experience I had on my way back to the bus stop from my dance class.

It’s 9:00 in the evening and I just spent the last hour and a half pushing myself as hard as I could in dance class. I was tired, I just wanted to sit quietly on the bus and rest. On my way back I walked past a beggar who asked for change. I’ve been trained that you don’t just give beggars money because many of them just use it to buy beer, or drugs, and such. So I said that I don’t have any change, which happened to be true, and kept walking.

Matthew 25:44-45:
44 Then they will reply, ‘Lord, when did we ever see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and not help you?’
45 And he will answer, ‘I tell you the truth, when you refused to help the least of these my brothers and sisters, you were refusing to help me.’

Then I got challenged by this idea. What ever you have done to the least of these, you have done to me. Surely not all beggars are trying to hustle me. So I stopped. I thought about it. Then the other part of what I’ve been trained came to mind. You don’t just give beggars money, but if they say they want the money for a cup of Tea or a sandwich for example, take them to get it. This also gives you the opportunity to talk with them as you go there and then sit and talk with them.

I was tired. But I knew what I needed to do. I turned around and walked back to him. I asked what he needed the money for, he said he wanted to get a cup of tea. I didn’t have change, but I had my debit card, I offered to take him to get a cup of Tea. He said, “No, that’s alright” and thanked me and I carried on my way praying for him.

Along my way I ran into another beggar and I mentally jumped out of my seat with excitement. I knew exactly what to say this time! He asked for change, I asked him what for and he told me, “for a sandwich.” to which I said, “I don’t have any change, but I can buy you a sandwich if you’d like.” He smiled and stood up and we walked to Tesco. We talked and he told me how he had a job and was starting the next morning and because he got a job he was going to be given a flat also. So this was to be his last night on the streets, but he just wanted some supper and had no money. I bought him the sandwich and he thanked me, I blessed him and sent him on his way.

I learned a few things from that interaction. Like I should have asked his name. Also I wish that I had prayed with him before we went our ways. I’ve not seen him since, so I can only assume that he is working at his new job. I pray that he’s doing well.

Coming off of that experience I felt great. He was hungry and I fed him! I’ve got this, I can totally do this. So I kept walking to my bus feeling pretty good and praying for the two chaps as I walked. Then God gave me one more wake up call. Just as I was coming around the last corner to my bus a third beggar pipes up and asks for change. I kept walking.

Matthew 23:27 – What sorrow awaits you teachers of religious law and you Pharisees. Hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs—beautiful on the outside but filled on the inside with dead people’s bones and all sorts of impurity.

My oh so peaceful mind, like a snow globe, was deeply focused on prayer. Then all of a sudden it was a whirl wind as if some one took the snow globe and shook it. I started complaining. Three?! Three beggars in one night. It was rare to even see more than one. It was late, I was tired, I’d already talked to two, I might miss my bus, I’d have to walk all the way back to Tesco. and on and on I went in my mind over this issue. But I never did go back to the third beggar. God reminded me loud and clear that it is not my strength that allows me to go out and do evangelism, but it is God through me that gives me strength even when I feel weak.

